### What is the database-per-service pattern?

Each microservice owns its data exclusively. Other services may read or change that data only by calling the owning service's API or by consuming its events, never by connecting to its database. This gives independent deployability, loose coupling, fault isolation, and the freedom to pick the best engine per service.

```java
package com.bank.account;

import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.GetMapping;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.PathVariable;
import org.springframework.web.bind.annotation.RestController;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@RestController
public class AccountController {

    private final AccountRepository repository;

    public AccountController(AccountRepository repository) {
        this.repository = repository;
    }

    // Other services call this API to get balance
    // They never connect to the account database directly
    @GetMapping("/api/accounts/{accountId}/balance")
    public BigDecimal getBalance(
            @PathVariable String accountId) {
        return repository.findBalanceByAccountId(accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Database-per-service rule:
  Account Service  -> owns accounts_db (PostgreSQL)
  Statement Service -> owns statements_db (Cassandra)
  Notification Service -> owns notifications_db (Redis)

  Statement Service needs Rajesh's account name?
  WRONG:  SELECT name FROM accounts_db.accounts  (direct DB access)
  RIGHT:  GET /api/accounts/SAV2001              (API call)
```

### When should you choose SQL?

Use SQL when you need strong consistency, ACID transactions, and complex relationships. The core ledger, accounts, and transfers where a lost or duplicated rupee is unacceptable demand PostgreSQL with real transactions.

```java
package com.bank.transfer;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferService {

    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public TransferService(AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional // ACID: both succeed or both roll back
    public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        BigDecimal fromBalance = accountRepo.getBalance(from);
        if (fromBalance.compareTo(amount) < 0) {
            throw new InsufficientFundsException(
                "Account " + from + " has insufficient funds");
        }
        accountRepo.debit(from, amount);
        accountRepo.credit(to, amount);
        // If credit fails, debit rolls back automatically
    }
}
```

```text
SQL strengths:
  ACID transactions: debit + credit in one atomic commit
  Foreign keys: accounts.customer_id -> customers.id
  Complex queries: JOIN accounts ON transfers WHERE date BETWEEN...
  Strong consistency: Rajesh always sees the correct balance
```

### When should you choose NoSQL?

Use NoSQL when scale, write throughput, flexible schema, or geographic distribution matters more than cross-entity transactions. Cassandra suits massive write-heavy, time-series data like transaction history.

```java
package com.bank.history;

import org.springframework.data.cassandra.core.mapping.Column;
import org.springframework.data.cassandra.core.mapping.PrimaryKey;
import org.springframework.data.cassandra.core.mapping.Table;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Instant;

@Table("transaction_history")
public class TransactionRecord {

    @PrimaryKey
    private TransactionKey key; // account_id + timestamp

    @Column("amount")
    private BigDecimal amount;

    @Column("type")
    private String type; // DEBIT, CREDIT

    @Column("description")
    private String description;

    // Cassandra: append-only, never updated, queried by
    // account + time range, horizontally scalable
}
```

```text
NoSQL strengths:
  Write throughput: 100K+ writes/sec across cluster
  Horizontal scale: add nodes to increase capacity linearly
  Flexible schema: new fields added without migration
  Tunable consistency: eventual for reads, quorum for writes
```

### When should you choose Redis?

Use Redis for anything that must be extremely fast and is either a cache or inherently ephemeral. Redis is not the source of truth; it sits in front of the real database with a TTL.

```java
package com.bank.cache;

import org.springframework.data.redis.core.StringRedisTemplate;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.Duration;

@Service
public class BalanceCacheService {

    private final StringRedisTemplate redis;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public BalanceCacheService(StringRedisTemplate redis,
                                AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.redis = redis;
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    // Cache-aside pattern
    public BigDecimal getBalance(String accountId) {
        String cached = redis.opsForValue().get("bal:" + accountId);
        if (cached != null) {
            return new BigDecimal(cached); // Cache hit
        }
        // Cache miss: read from PostgreSQL
        BigDecimal balance = accountRepo.findBalance(accountId);
        redis.opsForValue().set("bal:" + accountId,
            balance.toPlainString(), Duration.ofSeconds(30));
        return balance;
    }

    public void invalidate(String accountId) {
        redis.delete("bal:" + accountId);
    }
}
```

```text
Redis use cases:
  Cached balances:     TTL 30s, cache-aside
  Rate-limit counters: atomic INCR with EXPIRE
  Session data:        Spring Session with Redis
  Distributed locks:   Redisson or RedLock
  NOT source of truth: always backed by a real database
```

### How do the three compare?

| Factor | SQL (PostgreSQL) | NoSQL (Cassandra) | Redis |
|---|---|---|---|
| Consistency | Strong (ACID) | Tunable (eventual) | None (cache) |
| Scale | Vertical + replicas | Horizontal (linear) | In-memory (fast) |
| Query power | Joins, aggregates | Key-based lookups | Key-value only |
| Data shape | Structured, relational | Flexible, denormalized | Ephemeral |
| Use case | Accounts, transfers | History, audit logs | Cache, counters |
| Banking example | Rajesh's balance | Transaction history | Cached balance |

### What is polyglot persistence?

Polyglot persistence means each service uses the engine that best fits its workload. This is only possible with database-per-service, because once each service owns its store, you are free to choose the best engine.

```text
Polyglot persistence at National Bank of India:

  account-service       -> PostgreSQL  (ACID for money)
  transaction-history   -> Cassandra   (billions of append-only records)
  customer-profile      -> MongoDB     (flexible nested documents)
  notification-service  -> Redis       (ephemeral, fast)
  search-service        -> Elasticsearch (full-text search)
```

### What are the trade-offs of database-per-service?

You lose cross-service joins and distributed transactions. A business operation spanning two services becomes a Saga with compensating actions and eventual consistency.

```java
package com.bank.saga;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Service
public class TransferSaga {

    private final AccountServiceClient accountService;
    private final NotificationServiceClient notificationService;

    public TransferSaga(AccountServiceClient accountService,
                         NotificationServiceClient notificationService) {
        this.accountService = accountService;
        this.notificationService = notificationService;
    }

    public void execute(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        try {
            // Step 1: debit source (account-service owns this)
            accountService.debit(from, amount);

            // Step 2: credit destination (account-service owns this)
            accountService.credit(to, amount);

            // Step 3: notify (notification-service owns this)
            notificationService.sendTransferNotification(from, to, amount);
        } catch (Exception e) {
            // Compensate: reverse the debit
            accountService.credit(from, amount);
            throw new TransferFailedException("Transfer rolled back", e);
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Trade-offs of database-per-service:
  Lost:   Cross-service JOINs, distributed ACID transactions
  Gained: Independent deployment, fault isolation, polyglot persistence
  Handle: Sagas, events, eventual consistency, outbox pattern
```

### What are common mistakes when choosing databases?

The most common mistake is using NoSQL to avoid writing SQL. Another is putting money into an eventually-consistent store.

```text
WRONG decisions:
  Using MongoDB for account balances  -> eventual consistency loses money
  Using PostgreSQL for billions of logs -> does not scale horizontally
  Using Redis as source of truth       -> data lost on restart without persistence
  Using one DB for all services        -> tight coupling, no fault isolation

CORRECT decisions:
  PostgreSQL for money (strong consistency, ACID)
  Cassandra for history (write-heavy, append-only, scalable)
  Redis for caching (fast, ephemeral, TTL)
  MongoDB for profiles (flexible schema, nested documents)
```
